Antwerpenaar

Antwerpian

[LINK: (Sinjoor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Sinjoor&action=edit&redlink=1]  [ [LINK: (nl) -> https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinjoor] ] (colloquial)

Antwerp ( [LINK: (/ˈæntwɜːrp/) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/English]   [LINK: (ⓘ) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:En-us-Antwerp.ogg] ;  [LINK: (Dutch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_language] : Antwerpen  [LINK: ([ˈɑntʋɛrpə(n)]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/Dutch]   [LINK: (ⓘ) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Nl-Antwerpen.ogg] ;  [LINK: (French) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_language] : Anvers  [LINK: ([ɑ̃vɛʁs]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/French]   [LINK: (ⓘ) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:LL-Q150_(fra)-Jules78120-Anvers.wav] ) is a  [LINK: (city) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_status_in_Belgium]  and a  [LINK: (municipality) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Municipalities_of_Belgium]  in the  [LINK: (Flemish Region) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Region]  of  [LINK: (Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgium] . It is the capital and largest city of  [LINK: (Antwerp Province) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_Province] , and the third-largest municipality in Belgium by area at 208.22 km2 (80.39 sq mi). With a population of 565,039, it is the  [LINK: (most populous municipality) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_most_populous_municipalities_in_Belgium]  in Belgium, and with a metropolitan population of over 1.2 million people, the country's  [LINK: (second-largest metropolitan area) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_areas_in_Belgium]  after  [LINK: (Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels] . [LINK: ([a]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-fn1-2]  [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-BelMetr-3]

Flowing through Antwerp is the river  [LINK: (Scheldt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scheldt] . Antwerp is linked to the  [LINK: (North Sea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Sea]  by the river's  [LINK: (Westerschelde) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Scheldt]  estuary. It is about 40 km (25 mi) north of Brussels, and about 15 km (9 mi) south of the  [LINK: (Dutch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netherlands]  border. The  [LINK: (Port of Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_of_Antwerp]  is one of the biggest in the world, ranking second in Europe after  [LINK: (Rotterdam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rotterdam]  [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-4]  [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-5]  and  [LINK: (within the top 20 globally) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_world%27s_busiest_container_ports] . The city is also known as the hub of the world's  [LINK: (diamond trade) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond_industry] . In 2020, the  [LINK: (Globalization and World Cities Research Network) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Globalization_and_World_Cities_Research_Network]  rated Antwerp as a Gamma + (third level/top tier)  [LINK: (Global City) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_City] . [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-6]

Both economically and culturally, Antwerp is and has long been an important city in the  [LINK: (Low Countries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Low_Countries] , especially before and during the  [LINK: (Spanish Fury) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sack_of_Antwerp]  (1576) and throughout and after the subsequent  [LINK: (Dutch Revolt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_Revolt] . The  [LINK: (Stock Exchange in Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stock_Exchange,_Antwerp] , originally built in 1531 and re-built in 1872, was the world's first purpose-built  [LINK: (commodity exchange) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commodities_exchange] . [LINK: ([b]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-fn2-9]  In 1920, the city hosted the  [LINK: (Summer Olympics) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1920_Summer_Olympics] . [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-10]

The natives of Antwerp are nicknamed Sinjoren (Dutch pronunciation:  [LINK: ([sɪˈɲoːrə(n)]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/Dutch] ), after the Spanish honorific señor or French seigneur, "lord", referring to the Spanish noblemen who ruled the city in the 17th century. [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-11]  The city's population is very diverse, including about 180 nationalities; as of 2019, more than 50% of its population had a parent that was not a Belgian citizen at birth. [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-12]  A notable community is the  [LINK: (Jewish one) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jewish_Community_of_Antwerp] , as Antwerp is one of the only two cities in Europe (together with  [LINK: (London) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London]  and its  [LINK: (Stamford Hill) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stamford_Hill]  neighbourhood) that is home to a considerable  [LINK: (Haredi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haredi]  population in the 21st century.


# TOPONYMY #

Early recorded versions of the name include Ando Verpia on  [LINK: (Roman) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Rome]  coins found in the city centre, [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-belgiumview.com-13]  Germanic Andhunerbo from around the time  [LINK: (Austrasia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austrasia]  became a separate kingdom (that is, about 567 CE), [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-EB1911-14]  and (possibly originally Celtic) Andoverpis in  [LINK: (Dado) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Audoin_(bishop)] 's Life of  [LINK: (St. Eligius) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Eligius]  (Vita Eligii) from about 700 CE. The form Antverpia is  [LINK: (Neo-Latin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neo-Latin] . [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-15]

A Germanic ( [LINK: (Frankish) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankish_language]  or  [LINK: (Frisian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Frisian] ) origin could contain prefix anda ("against") and a noun derived from the verb werpen ("to throw") and denote, for example: land thrown up at the riverbank; an  [LINK: (alluvial deposit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alluvial_deposit] ; a mound (like a  [LINK: (terp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terp] ) thrown up (as a defence) against (something or someone); or a wharf. [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-16]  [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-placenames-17]  [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-Antwerp-18]  If Andoverpis is Celtic in origin, it could mean "those who live on both banks". [LINK: ([17]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-19]

There is a  [LINK: (folklore) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Folklore]  tradition that the name Antwerpen is from  [LINK: (Dutch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_language]  handwerpen ("hand-throwing"). A  [LINK: (giant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giant_(mythology)]  called  [LINK: (Antigoon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Druon_Antigoon]  is said to have lived near the  [LINK: (Scheldt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scheldt]  river and extracted a toll from passing boatmen. He severed the hand of anyone who did not pay, and threw it in the river. Eventually the giant was killed by a young hero named  [LINK: (Silvius Brabo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silvius_Brabo] , who cut off the giant's own hand and flung that into the river. This is unlikely to be the true origin, but it is celebrated by  [LINK: (a statue) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brabo_Fountain]  (illustrated further below) in the city's main market square, the  [LINK: (Grote Markt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grote_Markt_(Antwerp)] . [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-20]  [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-belgiumview.com-13]


# HISTORY #

Historical Antwerp allegedly had its origins in a Gallo-Roman  [LINK: (vicus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vicus_(Rome)] . Excavations carried out in the oldest section near the Scheldt river in 1952–1961 (ref. Princeton), produced pottery shards and fragments of glass from the mid-2nd century to the end of the 3rd century. In the 4th century, Antwerp was first named, having been settled by the  [LINK: (Germanic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germanic_peoples]   [LINK: (Franks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franks] . [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-Antwerp-18]

The  [LINK: (Merovingian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merovingian_dynasty]  Antwerp was evangelized by  [LINK: (Saint Amand) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Amand]  in the 7th century.  [LINK: (Het Steen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Het_Steen]  Castle has its origins in the  [LINK: (Carolingian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carolingian]  period in the 9th century. The castle may have been built after the Viking incursions in the early Middle Ages; in 879 the  [LINK: (Normans) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Normans]  invaded Flanders. The surviving structure was built between 1200 and 1225 as a gateway to a larger castle of the Dukes of Brabant which was demolished in the 19th century. It is Antwerp's oldest building. [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-21]  At the end of the 10th century, the Scheldt became the boundary of the  [LINK: (Holy Roman Empire) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_Roman_Empire] . Antwerp became a  [LINK: (margraviate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margraviate_of_Antwerp]  in 980, by the German emperor  [LINK: (Otto II) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_II,_Holy_Roman_Emperor] , a border province facing the  [LINK: (County of Flanders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/County_of_Flanders] .

In the 11th century, the best-known leader of the  [LINK: (First Crusade) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Crusade]  (1096–1099),  [LINK: (Godfrey of Bouillon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godfrey_of_Bouillon] , was originally  [LINK: (Margrave of Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margraviate_of_Antwerp#Margraves_of_Antwerp] , from 1076 until his death in 1100, though he was later also Duke of  [LINK: (Lower Lorraine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lower_Lorraine]  (1087–1100) and  [LINK: (Defender of the Holy Sepulchre) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_Jerusalem]  (1099–1100). In the 12th century,  [LINK: (Norbert of Xanten) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norbert_of_Xanten]  established a community of his  [LINK: (Premonstratensian canons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Premonstratensian]  at  [LINK: (St. Michael's Abbey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Michael%27s_Abbey,_Antwerp]  at Caloes. Antwerp was also the headquarters of  [LINK: (Edward III) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_III_of_England]  during his early negotiations with  [LINK: (Jacob van Artevelde) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_van_Artevelde] , and his son  [LINK: (Lionel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lionel_of_Antwerp,_1st_Duke_of_Clarence] , the  [LINK: (Duke of Clarence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duke_of_Clarence] , was born there in 1338. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-EB1911-14]

After the silting-up of the  [LINK: (Zwin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zwin]  and the consequent decline of  [LINK: (Bruges) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bruges] , Antwerp, then part of the  [LINK: (Duchy of Brabant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duchy_of_Brabant] , grew in importance, with the city doubling its population between 1500 and 1569. [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-:1-22]  At the end of the 15th century, the foreign trading houses were transferred from Bruges to Antwerp, and the building assigned to the association of English merchants active in the city is specifically mentioned in 1510. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-EB1911-14]  During this time, the old Mediterranean trade routes were gradually losing importance and the discovery of new sea routes via Africa to Asia and via the  [LINK: (Atlantic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Ocean]  to America helped push Antwerp to a position of prominence. [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-:1-22]

By 1504, the Portuguese had established Antwerp as one of their main shipping bases, bringing in spices from Asia and trading them for textiles and metal goods. The city's trade expanded to include cloth from England, Italy and Germany,  [LINK: (wines) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wine]  from Germany, France and Spain, salt from France, and  [LINK: (wheat) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheat]  from the  [LINK: (Baltic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baltic_Sea] . The city's skilled workers processed soap, fish, sugar, and especially cloth. Banks helped finance the trade, the merchants, and the manufacturers. The city was a cosmopolitan centre; its  [LINK: (Stock Exchange) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stock_Exchange,_Antwerp]  opened in 1531, "To the merchants of all nations." [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-23]

Antwerp became the sugar capital of Europe, importing the raw commodity from Portuguese and Spanish plantations on both sides of the Atlantic, where it was grown by a mixture of free and forced labour, increasingly with  [LINK: (enslaved Africans) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_slave_trade]  as the century progressed. [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-24]  The city attracted Italian and German sugar refiners by 1550, and shipped their refined product to Germany, especially  [LINK: (Cologne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cologne] . [LINK: ([23]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-25]  Antwerp also had an unusually high number of painters, around 360 in 1560, in a city with a population of roughly 89,000 in 1569 (250 people per painter), it was the most important artistic centre north of the  [LINK: (Alps) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alps] , serving notable painters such as  [LINK: (Pieter Bruegel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pieter_Bruegel_the_Elder] . [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-:1-22]  Moneylenders and financiers developed a large business lending money all over Europe including the English government in 1544–1574. London bankers were too small to operate on that scale, and Antwerp had a highly efficient stock exchange that itself attracted rich bankers from around Europe. After the 1570s, the city's banking business declined: England ceased its borrowing in Antwerp in 1574. [LINK: ([24]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-26]

[LINK: (Fernand Braudel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fernand_Braudel]  states that Antwerp became "the centre of the entire international economy, something Bruges had never been even at its height." [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-27]  Antwerp had the highest growth rate and was the richest city in Europe at the time. [LINK: ([26]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-world_and_its_people-28]  [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-:1-22]  Antwerp's  [LINK: (Golden Age) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Age]  is tightly linked to the " [LINK: (Age of Exploration) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Age_of_Discovery] ". During the first half of the 16th century, Antwerp grew to become the second-largest European city north of the Alps. Many foreign merchants were resident in the city.  [LINK: (Francesco Guicciardini) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francesco_Guicciardini] , the Florentine envoy, stated that hundreds of ships would pass in a day, and 2,000 carts entered the city each week. Portuguese ships laden with  [LINK: (pepper) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_pepper]  and  [LINK: (cinnamon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinnamon]  would unload their cargo. According to Luc-Normand Tellier "It is estimated that the port of Antwerp was earning the Spanish crown seven times more revenues than the Spanish colonization of the Americas". [LINK: ([27]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-golden_age-29]

Without a long-distance merchant fleet, and governed by an oligarchy of banker-aristocrats forbidden to engage in trade, the economy of Antwerp was foreign-controlled, which made the city very cosmopolitan, with merchants and traders from  [LINK: (Venice) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venetian_Republic] ,  [LINK: (Genoa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_Genoa] ,  [LINK: (Ragusa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_Ragusa] ,  [LINK: (Spain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spain]  and Portugal. Antwerp had a policy of toleration, which attracted a large crypto- [LINK: (Jewish) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Jews_in_Antwerp]  community composed of migrants from Spain and Portugal. [LINK: ([29]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-31]

Antwerp experienced three booms during its golden age: the first based on the pepper market, a second launched by American silver coming from  [LINK: (Seville) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seville]  (ending with the bankruptcy of Spain in 1557), and a third boom, after the stabilizing  [LINK: (Treaty of Cateau-Cambresis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_War_of_1551%E2%80%931559]  in 1559, based on the textiles industry. At the beginning of the 16th century, Antwerp accounted for 40% of world trade. [LINK: ([27]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-golden_age-29]  The boom-and-bust cycles and inflationary cost-of-living squeezed less-skilled workers. In the century after 1541, the city's economy and population declined dramatically. The Portuguese merchants left in 1549, and there was much less trade in English cloth. Numerous financial bankruptcies began around 1557.  [LINK: (Amsterdam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amsterdam]  replaced Antwerp as the major trading centre for the region. [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-32]

The religious revolution of the  [LINK: (Reformation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protestant_Reformation]  erupted in violent riots in August 1566, as in other parts of the  [LINK: (Low Countries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Low_Countries] . The regent  [LINK: (Margaret, Duchess of Parma) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_of_Parma] , was swept aside when  [LINK: (Philip II) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_II_of_Spain]  sent the  [LINK: (Duke of Alba) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fernando_%C3%81lvarez_de_Toledo,_3rd_Duke_of_Alba]  at the head of an army the following summer. When the  [LINK: (Dutch revolt against Spain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War]  broke out in 1568, commercial trading between Antwerp and the Spanish port of  [LINK: (Bilbao) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bilbao]  collapsed and became impossible. On 4 November 1576, Spanish soldiers sacked the city during the so-called  [LINK: (Spanish Fury) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sack_of_Antwerp] : 8,000 citizens were massacred, several houses burnt down, and over £2 million sterling of damage was done.

Subsequently, the city joined the  [LINK: (Union of Utrecht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union_of_Utrecht]  in 1579 and became the capital of the  [LINK: (Dutch Revolt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_Revolt] . In 1585,  [LINK: (Alessandro Farnese, Duke of Parma and Piacenza) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Farnese,_Duke_of_Parma] , captured it after a  [LINK: (long siege) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_of_Antwerp_(1584%E2%80%931585)]  and as part of the terms of surrender its  [LINK: (Protestant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protestantism]  citizens were given two years to settle their affairs before quitting the city. [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-33]  Most went to the  [LINK: (United Provinces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_Republic]  in the north, starting the  [LINK: (Dutch Golden Age) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_Golden_Age] . Antwerp's banking was controlled for a generation by  [LINK: (Genoa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genoa] , and  [LINK: (Amsterdam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amsterdam]  became the new trading centre.

The recognition of the independence of the  [LINK: (United Provinces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_Republic]  by the  [LINK: (Treaty of Münster) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peace_of_M%C3%BCnster]  in 1648 stipulated that the  [LINK: (Scheldt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scheldt]  should be closed to navigation, which destroyed Antwerp's trading activities. This impediment remained in force until 1863, although the provisions were relaxed during French rule from 1795 to 1814, and also during the time Belgium formed part of the  [LINK: (Kingdom of the United Netherlands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom_of_the_Netherlands]  (1815 to 1830). [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-EB1911-14]  Antwerp had reached the lowest point in its fortunes in 1800, and its population had sunk to under 40,000, when  [LINK: (Napoleon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Napoleon_I_of_France] , realizing its strategic importance, assigned funds to enlarge the harbour by constructing a new dock (still named the Bonaparte Dock), an  [LINK: (access-lock) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lock_(water_transport)]  and  [LINK: (mole) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mole_(architecture)] , and deepening the Scheldt to allow larger ships to approach Antwerp. [LINK: ([26]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-world_and_its_people-28]  Napoleon hoped that by making Antwerp's harbour the finest in Europe he would be able to counter the Port of London and hamper British growth. However, he was defeated at the  [LINK: (Battle of Waterloo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Waterloo]  before he could see the plan through. [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-world_and_its_people2-34] 
In 1830, the city was captured by the Belgian insurgents, but the citadel continued to be held by a Dutch garrison under General  [LINK: (David Hendrik Chassé) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Hendrik_Chass%C3%A9] . For a time, Chassé subjected the town to periodic bombardment which inflicted much damage, and at the end of 1832, the citadel itself was besieged by the French Northern Army commanded by Marechal  [LINK: (Gerard) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerard] . During this attack, the town was further damaged. In December 1832, after a gallant defence, Chassé made an honourable surrender, ending the  [LINK: (Siege of Antwerp (1832)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Antwerp_(1832)] . [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-EB1911-14]

Later that century, a double ring of  [LINK: (Brialmont) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brialmont]  Fortresses was constructed some 10 km (6 mi) from the city centre, as Antwerp was considered vital for the survival of the young Belgian state. And in 1894 Antwerp presented itself to the world via a  [LINK: (World's Fair) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_International_Exposition_(1894)]  attended by 3 million. [LINK: ([33]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-35]

Antwerp was the first city to host the  [LINK: (World Gymnastics Championships) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Gymnastics_Championships] , in 1903. During  [LINK: (World War I) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_I] , the city became the fallback point of the  [LINK: (Belgian Army) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_Army]  after the defeat at  [LINK: (Liège) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li%C3%A8ge] . The  [LINK: (Siege of Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Antwerp_(1914)]  lasted for 11 days, but the city was taken after heavy fighting by the German Army, and the Belgians were forced to retreat westwards. Antwerp remained under  [LINK: (German occupation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_occupation_of_Belgium_during_World_War_I]  until the Armistice. A few years later, Antwerp hosted the  [LINK: (1920 Summer Olympics) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1920_Summer_Olympics] .

During  [LINK: (World War II) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_II] , the city was an important strategic target because of its port. It was  [LINK: (occupied by Germany) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_Administration_in_Belgium_and_Northern_France]  on 18 May 1940 and liberated by the  [LINK: (British 11th Armoured Division) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/11th_Armoured_Division_(United_Kingdom)]  on 4 September 1944. After this, the Germans attempted to destroy the  [LINK: (Port of Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_of_Antwerp] , which was used by the Allies to bring new material ashore. Thousands of  [LINK: (Rheinbote) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rheinbote] ,  [LINK: (V-1) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V-1_flying_bomb]  and  [LINK: (V-2) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V-2]  missiles were fired (more V-2s than used on all other targets during the entire war combined), causing severe damage to the city but failed to destroy the port due to poor accuracy. After the war, Antwerp, which had already had a sizeable Jewish population before the war, once again became a major European centre of  [LINK: (Haredi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haredi_Judaism]  (and particularly  [LINK: (Hasidic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hasidic_Judaism] )  [LINK: (Orthodox Judaism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orthodox_Judaism] .

A Ten-Year Plan for the port of Antwerp (1956–1965) expanded and modernized the port's infrastructure with national funding to build a set of canal docks. The broader aim was to facilitate the growth of the north-eastern Antwerp metropolitan region, which attracted new industry based on a flexible and strategic implementation of the project as a co-production between various authorities and private parties. The plan succeeded in extending the linear layout along the Scheldt river by connecting new satellite communities to the main strip. [LINK: ([34]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-36]

Starting in the 1990s, Antwerp rebranded itself as a world-class fashion centre. Emphasizing the avant-garde, it tried to compete with  [LINK: (London) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London] ,  [LINK: (Milan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milan] ,  [LINK: (New York) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City]  and  [LINK: (Paris) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris] . It emerged from organized tourism and mega-cultural events. [LINK: ([35]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-37]


# DISTRICTS #

The municipality comprises the city of Antwerp proper and several towns. It is divided into ten entities (districts):

(km2)

in residential areas

In 1958, in preparation of the 10-year development plan for the  [LINK: (Port of Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_of_Antwerp] , the municipalities of  [LINK: (Berendrecht-Zandvliet-Lillo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berendrecht-Zandvliet-Lillo]  were integrated into the city territory and lost their administrative independence. During the 1983 merger of municipalities, conducted by the Belgian government as an administrative simplification, the municipalities of  [LINK: (Berchem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berchem] ,  [LINK: (Borgerhout) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borgerhout] ,  [LINK: (Deurne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deurne,_Belgium] ,  [LINK: (Ekeren) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ekeren] ,  [LINK: (Hoboken) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hoboken,_Antwerp] ,  [LINK: (Merksem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merksem]  and  [LINK: (Wilrijk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilrijk]  were merged into the city. At that time the city was also divided into the districts mentioned above. Simultaneously, districts received an appointed district council; later district councils became elected bodies. [LINK: ([36]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-38]

The neighbouring municipality of  [LINK: (Borsbeek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borsbeek]  declared the intention to become the tenth district of the city of Antwerp. Since January 2025, Borsbeek has been a district of the city of Antwerp. [LINK: ([37]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-39]

With the exception of Ekeren and Berendrecht-Zandvliet-Lillo, all the districts form together one contiguous inhabited area. The former town halls have been converted into district halls and the former town centres are now local main streets within the larger urban agglomeration.


