Berchem - 2600

[LINK: (Antwerp-Berchem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berchem] : Vibrant district, rich history, lively streets, major transportation hub.


# CITYSCAPE AND ARCHITECTURE #

Antwerp's architecture is a blend of old and new, with a cultural heritage spanning from the Gothic and Renaissance periods to contemporary designs. In the 16th century, the city was noted for the wealth of its citizens (Antwerpia nummis). [LINK: ([38]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-40]  The houses of these wealthy merchants and manufacturers have been preserved throughout the city. However, fire has destroyed several old buildings, such as the house of the  [LINK: (Hanseatic League) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanseatic_League]  on the northern quays, in 1891. During  [LINK: (World War II) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_II] , the city also suffered considerable damage from  [LINK: (V-bombs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vergeltungswaffe] . Here are some of the most notable examples of Antwerp's diverse architecture:

[LINK: (The Port House) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_Authority_Building_(Antwerp)] , designed by  [LINK: (Zaha Hadid) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaha_Hadid] , is a contemporary landmark that juxtaposes a restored neoclassical building with a futuristic glass-clad extension.

The Provincial Government Building, designed by  [LINK: (Xaveer De Geyter) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xaveer_De_Geyter]  Architects, is a unique triangular-shaped building with a natural stone façade located in the historic city centre.

[LINK: (De Singel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DeSingel] , a cultural centre with a modernist design, features a mix of glass and concrete and is considered an architectural marvel.

[LINK: (The Palace of Justice) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palace_of_Justice_Antwerp] , designed by  [LINK: (Richard Rogers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Rogers] , is a modern building with a prominent dome and makes extensive use of glass and steel.

The  [LINK: (MAS Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_aan_de_Stroom] , with its distinctive red sandstone façade, is a contemporary building that explores Antwerp's rich maritime history.

The  [LINK: (Zurenborg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zurenborg]  neighbourhood is known for its Art Nouveau architecture, featuring intricate details, colourful mosaics, and stained glass windows.

[LINK: (Maison Guiette) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maison_Guiette]  is a residential building designed by the Swiss-French architect  [LINK: (Le Corbusier) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Corbusier]  in collaboration with the Belgian architect Paul De Meyer in 1926–1927. Maison Guiette is considered an early example of Le Corbusier's work in Europe. It is now listed as a UNESCO World Heritage site and is recognized as an important example of modernist architecture.

De  [LINK: (Boerentoren) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boerentoren] , or "The Farmer's Tower", is a historic Art Deco skyscraper that was once the tallest building in Europe.

The  [LINK: (Bourla Theatre) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bourla_Theatre] , with its Neo-Classical style, is a historic theatre that dates back to the 19th century.

The  [LINK: (Hendrik Conscience Heritage Library) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hendrik_Conscience_Heritage_Library]  is a historic library. The library was established in 1481 and is one of the oldest and most extensive heritage libraries in Belgium. It is home to over 1.5 million books, manuscripts, and other documents related to the history and culture of Flanders.

The  [LINK: (Royal Museum of Fine Arts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Antwerp] , with its Neo-Classical style, houses a vast collection of Flemish art from the 14th to the 20th century.

[LINK: (Antwerp Central Station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerpen-Centraal_railway_station] , a beautiful Beaux-Arts railway station, is one of the most impressive examples of railway architecture in Europe.

The Sint-Annatunnel, [LINK: ([39]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-41]  also known as the Voetgangerstunnel, is a 572-meter-long tunnel. It was opened in 1933 and was designed by engineer  [LINK: (Emiel Van Averbeke) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89mile_Van_Averbeke] . The tunnel connects the banks of the Scheldt River, linking the historic city centre with the Left Bank neighbourhood. It is primarily made of reinforced concrete and features an Art Deco style interior design with ceramic tiles, ornamental lighting fixtures, and decorative panels. One of its unique features is the wooden escalator, which has been in operation since the tunnel's opening. Today, the Voetgangerstunnel is a popular tourist attraction and a key transportation link for commuters and visitors to Antwerp.

Nieuw Zuid (New South) neighbourhood. Located next to the river on the site of abandoned railway tracks, Nieuw Zuid is being designed as a sustainable green neighbourhood. The project, which will not be finished until 2030, includes buildings by acclaimed international architects such as  [LINK: (Max Dudler) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Max_Dudler] ,  [LINK: (Stefano Boeri) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stefano_Boeri] ,  [LINK: (Peter Zumthor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Zumthor] ,  [LINK: (Shigeru Ban) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shigeru_Ban]  and  [LINK: (Kazuyo Sejima) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kazuyo_Sejima] .

Antwerp is also home to a wealth of historic Gothic, Baroque and Renaissance-style buildings, such as the  [LINK: (Antwerp City Hall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_City_Hall] , the  [LINK: (Cathedral of Our Lady) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathedral_of_Our_Lady_(Antwerp)] , the  [LINK: (St. Paul's Church) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Paul%27s_Church,_Antwerp] , the  [LINK: (St. James' Church) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._James%27_Church,_Antwerp] , the  [LINK: (St. Andrew's Church) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Andrew%27s_Church,_Antwerp] ,  the  [LINK: (St. Charles Borromeo Church) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Charles_Borromeo_Church,_Antwerp]  and the  [LINK: (Vleeshuis Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vleeshuis] .

[LINK: (Antwerp City Hall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_City_Hall]  at the  [LINK: (Grote Markt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grote_Markt_(Antwerp)]

16th-century  [LINK: (guildhalls) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guildhall]  at the Grote Markt

