In 2010, 36% to 39% of the inhabitants of Antwerp had foreign origins. A study projected that in 2020, 55% of the population would be of immigrant background, either first, second, or third generation.[ [LINK: (needs update) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Manual_of_Style/Dates_and_numbers#Chronological_items] ] [LINK: ([57]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-59]  [LINK: ([58]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-60]

After  [LINK: (the Holocaust) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Holocaust]  and the murder of its many Jews, Antwerp became a major centre for Orthodox Jews. At present, about 15,000  [LINK: (Haredi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haredi_Judaism]  Jews, many of them  [LINK: (Hasidic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hasidic_Judaism] , live in Antwerp. The city has three official Jewish Congregations: Shomrei Hadass, headed by Rabbi Pinchas Padwa, Machsike Hadass, headed by Rabbi Aron Schiff (formerly by Chief Rabbi  [LINK: (Chaim Kreiswirth) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chaim_Kreiswirth] ) and the Portuguese Community Ben Moshe. Antwerp has an extensive network of synagogues, shops, schools and organizations. Significant Hasidic movements in Antwerp include  [LINK: (Pshevorsk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pshevorsk_(Hasidic_dynasty)] , based in Antwerp, as well as branches of  [LINK: (Satmar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satmar_(Hasidic_dynasty)] ,  [LINK: (Belz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belz_(Hasidic_dynasty)] ,  [LINK: (Bobov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bobov_(Hasidic_dynasty)] ,  [LINK: (Ger) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ger_(Hasidic_dynasty)] ,  [LINK: (Skver) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skver_(Hasidic_dynasty)] ,  [LINK: (Klausenburg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Klausenburg_(Hasidic_dynasty)] ,  [LINK: (Vizhnitz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vizhnitz_(Hasidic_dynasty)]  and several others. Rabbi  [LINK: (Chaim Kreiswirth) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chaim_Kreiswirth] , chief rabbi of the Machsike Hadas community, who died in 2001, was arguably one of the better known personalities to have been based in Antwerp. An attempt to have a street named after him has received the support of the Town Hall and is in the process of being implemented.[ [LINK: (citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed] ]

The Jains in Belgium are estimated to be around about 1,500 people. The majority live in Antwerp, mostly involved in the very lucrative  [LINK: (diamond) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond]  business. [LINK: ([60]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-65]  Belgian Indian Jains control two-thirds of the rough diamonds trade and supplied India with roughly 36% of their rough diamonds. [LINK: ([61]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-66]  A major temple, with a cultural centre, has been built in Antwerp (Wilrijk). Ramesh Mehta, a Jain, is a member of the Belgian Council of Religious Leaders, put up on 17 December 2009.[ [LINK: (citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed] ]

Significant  [LINK: (Armenian communities) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armenians]  reside in Antwerp, many of whom are descendants of traders who settled during the 19th century. Most Armenian Belgians are adherents of the  [LINK: (Armenian Apostolic Church) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armenian_Apostolic_Church] , while a smaller number are adherents of the  [LINK: (Armenian Catholic Church) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armenian_Catholic_Church]  and  [LINK: (Armenian Evangelical Church) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armenian_Evangelical_Church] .

One of the important sectors that Armenian communities in Antwerp excel at and are involved in is the  [LINK: (diamond) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond]  trade business, [LINK: ([62]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-67]  [LINK: ([63]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-68]  [LINK: ([64]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-69]  [LINK: ([65]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-70]  that based primarily in the  [LINK: (diamond district) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_diamond_district] . [LINK: ([66]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-guardian.co.uk-71]  [LINK: ([67]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-72]  [LINK: ([68]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-73]  Some of the famous Armenian families involved in the diamond business in the city are the Artinians, Arslanians, Aslanians, Barsamians and the Osganians. [LINK: ([69]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-74]


# ECONOMY #

According to the  [LINK: (American Association of Port Authorities) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Association_of_Port_Authorities] , the  [LINK: (port of Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_of_Antwerp]  was the seventeenth largest port in the world in 2005 (by tonnage), and second only to  [LINK: (Rotterdam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_of_Rotterdam]  in Europe. It handled 235.2 million tons of cargo in 2018. Importantly it handles high volumes of economically attractive  [LINK: (general) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cargo]  and  [LINK: (project cargo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heavy_lift] , as well as  [LINK: (bulk cargo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulk_cargo] . Antwerp's docklands, with five  [LINK: (oil refineries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oil_refinery] , are home to a massive concentration of  [LINK: (petrochemical) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petrochemical]  industries, second only to the petrochemical cluster in  [LINK: (Houston) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Houston] , Texas. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-75]   [LINK: (Electricity generation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electricity_generation]  is also an important activity, with four  [LINK: (nuclear power) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_power]  plants at  [LINK: (Doel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doel] , a  [LINK: (conventional power station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fossil-fuel_power_plant]  in Kallo, as well as several smaller  [LINK: (combined cycle) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combined_cycle]  plants. There is a  [LINK: (wind farm) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_farm]  in the northern part of the port area. There are plans to extend this in the period 2014–2020. [LINK: ([71]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-sustainableportofantwerp-76]  The old Belgian  [LINK: (bluestone) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bluestone]   [LINK: (quays) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quay]  bordering the  [LINK: (Scheldt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scheldt]  for a distance of 5.6 km (3.5 mi) to the north and south of the city centre have been retained for their sentimental value and are used mainly by  [LINK: (cruise ships) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cruise_ship]  and  [LINK: (short sea shipping) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Short_sea_shipping] .[ [LINK: (citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed] ]

Antwerp's other great mainstay is the diamond trade that takes place largely within the  [LINK: (diamond district) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_diamond_district] . [LINK: ([72]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-NYT11512-77]  85 percent of the world's rough diamonds pass through the district annually, [LINK: ([73]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-:0-78]  and in 2011 turnover in the industry was $56 billion. [LINK: ([74]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-79]  The city has four  [LINK: (diamond bourses) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond#Distribution] : the Diamond Club of Antwerp, the Beurs voor Diamanthandel, the  [LINK: (Antwerpsche Diamantkring) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerpsche_Diamantkring]  and the Vrije Diamanthandel. [LINK: ([75]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-awdc-80]

Antwerp's history in the diamond trade dates back to as early as the sixteenth century, [LINK: ([73]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-:0-78]  with the first diamond cutters guild being introduced in 1584. The industry never disappeared from Antwerp, and even experienced a second boom in the early twentieth century. By the year 1924, Antwerp had over 13,000 diamond finishers. [LINK: ([76]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-81]  Since World War II families of the large  [LINK: (Hasidic Jewish community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jewish_Community_of_Antwerp]  have dominated Antwerp's diamond trading industry, although the last two decades have seen  [LINK: (Indian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indians_in_Belgium]  [LINK: ([77]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-Stefangeens.com-82]  and  [LINK: (Maronite) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maronites]   [LINK: (Christians) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christians]  from  [LINK: (Lebanon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebanon]  and  [LINK: (Armenian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armenians] , [LINK: ([66]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-guardian.co.uk-71]  traders become increasingly important. [LINK: ([77]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-Stefangeens.com-82]  The district was the site of the 2003  [LINK: (Antwerp diamond heist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_diamond_heist] , the largest diamond theft of all time.

[LINK: (Antwerp World Diamond Centre) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_World_Diamond_Centre] , (AWDC) the successor to the Hoge Raad voor Diamant, plays an important role in setting standards, regulating professional ethics, training and promoting the interests of Antwerp as the capital of the diamond industry. [LINK: ([78]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-83]

In recent years Antwerp has seen a downturn in the diamond business, with the industry shifting to cheaper labor markets such as Dubai or India. [LINK: ([79]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-84]  The industry has avoided the  [LINK: (2022 European sanctions against Russia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_sanctions_during_the_2022_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine]  although the imports from  [LINK: (Alrosa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alrosa]  have diminished. If banned, the AWDC claims 10,000 jobs would be at risk. [LINK: ([80]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-Rankin-85]


# TRANSPORTATION #

Antwerp is the focus of lines to the north to Essen and the Netherlands, east to Turnhout, south to Mechelen, Brussels and Charleroi, and southwest to Ghent and Ostend. It is served by international and  [LINK: (Thalys) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thalys]  trains to  [LINK: (Amsterdam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amsterdam] ,  [LINK: (Rotterdam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rotterdam]  and  [LINK: (Paris) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris] , and national trains to  [LINK: (Ghent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghent] ,  [LINK: (Bruges) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bruges] ,  [LINK: (Ostend) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostend] ,  [LINK: (Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels] ,  [LINK: (Charleroi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charleroi] ,  [LINK: (Hasselt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hasselt] ,  [LINK: (Liège) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li%C3%A8ge] ,  [LINK: (Leuven) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leuven]  and  [LINK: (Turnhout) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turnhout] .  [LINK: (Antwerp Central station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerpen-Central_railway_station]  is an architectural monument in itself, and is mentioned in  [LINK: (W G Sebald) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W._G._Sebald] 's novel  [LINK: (Austerlitz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austerlitz_(novel)] . Prior to the completion in 2007 of a tunnel that runs northwards under the city centre to emerge at the old Antwerp Dam station, Central was a terminus. Trains from Brussels to the Netherlands had to either reverse at Central or call only at Berchem station, 2 km (1 mi) to the south, and then describe a semicircle to the east, round the Singel. Now, they call at the new lower level of the station before continuing in the same direction.

Antwerp is also home to Antwerpen-Noord, the largest  [LINK: (classification yard) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classification_yard]  for freight in Belgium and the second largest in Europe. The majority of freight trains in Belgium depart from or arrive here. It has two classification humps and over a hundred tracks.

The city has a web of tram and bus lines operated by  [LINK: (De Lijn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Lijn]  and providing access to the city centre, suburbs and the Left Bank. The  [LINK: (tram network) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_Tram]  has 14 lines, of which the underground section is called the " [LINK: (premetro) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_Pre-metro] " and includes a tunnel under the river. The  [LINK: (Franklin Rooseveltplaats) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franklin_Rooseveltplaats]  functions as the city's main hub for local and regional bus lines. However, there has been an evolution to end regional lines at transportation hubs more outward of the city centre, such as Zuid and Luchtbal.

A six-lane motorway bypass encircles much of the city centre and runs through the urban residential area of Antwerp. Known locally as the "Ring" it offers motorway connections to  [LINK: (Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels] ,  [LINK: (Hasselt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hasselt]  and  [LINK: (Liège) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li%C3%A8ge] ,  [LINK: (Ghent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghent] ,  [LINK: (Lille) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lille]  and  [LINK: (Bruges) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bruges]  and  [LINK: (Breda) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breda]  and  [LINK: (Bergen op Zoom) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bergen_op_Zoom]  (Netherlands). The banks of the Scheldt are linked by three road tunnels (in order of construction): the Waasland Tunnel (1934), the  [LINK: (Kennedy Tunnel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kennedytunnel]  (1967) and the  [LINK: (Liefkenshoek Tunnel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liefkenshoektunnel]  (1991).

Daily congestion on the Ring led to a fourth high-volume highway link called the " [LINK: (Oosterweelconnection) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oosterweelconnection] " being proposed. It would have entailed the construction of a long viaduct and bridge (the Lange Wapper) over the docks on the north side of the city in combination with the widening of the existing motorway into a 14-lane motorway; these plans were eventually rejected in a 2009 public referendum. [LINK: ([81]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-86]

In September 2010 the  [LINK: (Flemish Government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Government]  decided to replace the bridge by a series of tunnels. There are ideas to cover the Ring in a similar way as happened around Paris,  [LINK: (Hamburg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamburg] ,  [LINK: (Madrid) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madrid]  and other cities. This would reconnect the city with its suburbs and would provide development opportunities to accommodate part of the foreseen population growth in Antwerp which currently are not possible because of the pollution and noise generated by the traffic on the Ring. An old plan to build an R2 outer ring road outside the built-up urban area around the Antwerp agglomeration for port-related traffic and transit traffic never materialized. [ [LINK: (citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed] ]

In 2017 it was finally decided to complete the ringway of Antwerp with a new tunnel crossing the Scheldt. In a compromise with civil society, it has equally been decided that the ringway will in the long term completely be covered. This compromise is called the Toekomstverbond ("Agreement for the future"). Part of the same agreement is that highways further away from the city centre will be further developed and that the number of motorized trips in all transportation should be reduced to 50%.

Many  [LINK: (FlixBus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FlixBus] es have a stop at the central Franklin Rooseveltplaats.

A small airport,  [LINK: (Antwerp International Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_International_Airport] , is located in the district of  [LINK: (Deurne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deurne,_Belgium] . The airport is mainly used for business travel. Flying Group, the largest business jet service in Benelux and France, has its headquarters there and several maintenance sheds and hangars for their private jets. There are also some regularly scheduled flights by  [LINK: (TUI fly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TUI_fly_Belgium]  with direct connectivity to Spain, Croatia, Italy, Austria and Morocco.  [LINK: (Sky Alps) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky_Alps]  operates a 2-3x weekly connection to  [LINK: (Bolzano Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bolzano_Airport] . A bus service connects the airport to the city centre. The runway has increased in length, and there is now direct connectivity to Spain, Germany, France, Italy, and Greece from the city of Antwerp.

Belgium's major international airport,  [LINK: (Brussels Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Airport] , is about 45 km (28 mi) from the city of Antwerp, and connects the city worldwide. It is connected to the city centre by bus, and also by train. The new  [LINK: (Diabolo rail connection) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diabolo_project]  provides a direct fast train connection between Antwerp and Brussels Airport as of the summer of 2012.

There is also a direct rail service between Antwerp (calling at Central and Berchem stations) and  [LINK: (Charleroi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charleroi]  South station, with a connecting buslink to  [LINK: (Brussels South Charleroi Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_South_Charleroi_Airport] , which runs twice per hour on working days.

Since 2017 there has been a regular water ferry halting at the city centre, connecting it with towns up and down the river Scheldt from  [LINK: (Kruibeke) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hemiksem]  to Lillo. This service is called DeWaterbus. It is popular with both commuting port laborers and cyclist making a day trip.


# POLITICS #

The current city council was elected in the  [LINK: (2024 local elections) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Belgian_local_elections] , with its governing coalition consisting of the parties  [LINK: (New Flemish Alliance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Flemish_Alliance]  and  [LINK: (Vooruit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vooruit_(political_party)] , led by acting mayor  [LINK: (Els van Doesburg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Els_van_Doesburg]  (N-VA). [LINK: ([82]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-87]  [LINK: ([83]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-88]

In the 16th and 17th century important mayors include  [LINK: (Philips of Marnix, Lord of Saint-Aldegonde) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philips_of_Marnix,_Lord_of_Saint-Aldegonde] ,  [LINK: (Anthony van Stralen, Lord of Merksem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_van_Stralen,_Lord_of_Merksem]  and  [LINK: (Nicolaas II Rockox) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicolaas_II_Rockox] . In the early years after Belgian independence, Antwerp was governed by Catholic-Unionist mayors. Between 1848 and 1921, all mayors were from the Liberal Party (except for the so-called Meeting-intermezzo between 1863 and 1872). Between 1921 and 1932, the city had a Catholic mayor again: Frans Van Cauwelaert. From 1932 onwards and up until 2013, all mayors belonged to the  [LINK: (Social Democrat) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Socialistische_Partij_Anders]  party:  [LINK: (Camille Huysmans) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camille_Huysmans] ,  [LINK: (Lode Craeybeckx) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lode_Craeybeckx] , Frans Detiège and  [LINK: (Mathilde Schroyens) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathilde_Schroyens] , and after the municipality fusion:  [LINK: (Bob Cools) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Bob_Cools&action=edit&redlink=1]  [ [LINK: (nl) -> https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bob_Cools] ],  [LINK: (Leona Detiège) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leona_Deti%C3%A8ge]  and  [LINK: (Patrick Janssens) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick_Janssens] . Since February 2025, the acting mayor is the Flemish nationalist Els van Doesburg, belonging to the  [LINK: (Flemish separatist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Movement]  party  [LINK: (New Flemish Alliance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Flemish_Alliance] . [LINK: ([84]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-89]


# CULTURE #

Antwerp had an artistic reputation in the 17th century, based on its  [LINK: (school of painting) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_school] , which included  [LINK: (Rubens) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Paul_Rubens] ,  [LINK: (Van Dyck) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_van_Dyck] ,  [LINK: (Jordaens) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_Jordaens] , the  [LINK: (Teniers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teniers]  and many others. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-EB1911-14]

[LINK: (Royal Museum of Fine Arts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Museum_of_Fine_Arts,_Antwerp] . The  [LINK: (neoclassical) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neoclassical_architecture]  building housing a collection of  [LINK: (paintings) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Painting] ,  [LINK: (sculptures) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sculpture]  and  [LINK: (drawings) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drawing]  from the 14th to the 20th centuries. It is one of the primary landmarks of the  [LINK: (Zuid) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zuid_(Antwerp)]  district of Antwerp. The majestic building was designed by  [LINK: (Jean-Jacques Winders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Jacques_Winders]  and  [LINK: (Frans Van Dijk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frans_Van_Dijk] , built beginning in 1884, opened in 1890, and completed in 1894.

[LINK: (Rubenshuis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubenshuis] , is the former home and studio of  [LINK: (Peter Paul Rubens) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Paul_Rubens]  in Antwerp. It is now a museum.

[LINK: (Plantin-Moretus Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plantin-Moretus_Museum] , [LINK: ([85]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-90]  preserves the house of the printer  [LINK: (Christoffel Plantijn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christophe_Plantin]  and his successor  [LINK: (Jan Moretus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jan_Moretus] . It has been a  [LINK: (UNESCO World Heritage Site) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNESCO_World_Heritage_Site]  since 2005. [LINK: ([86]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-91]

[LINK: (Museum Mayer van den Bergh) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_Mayer_van_den_Bergh] , with works from the Gothic and Renaissance period in the Netherlands and Belgium, including paintings by  [LINK: (Pieter Brueghel the Elder) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pieter_Brueghel_the_Elder] .

[LINK: (Snijders&Rockox House) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snijders%26Rockox_House]  is located in two neighbouring townhouses and showcases a collection of 16th and 17th century Flemish art.

[LINK: (Museum of Contemporary Art (M HKA)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_of_Contemporary_Art,_Antwerp] , [LINK: ([87]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-92]  the museum holds a permanent collection of contemporary art from Belgian and international artists, an arthouse cinema and an extensive library of books on contemporary art.

[LINK: (Museum aan de Stroom) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_aan_de_Stroom] , whose central focus is Antwerp and its connection to the world.

[LINK: (Fotomuseum Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fotomuseum_Antwerp] , [LINK: ([88]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-93]  also known as FOMU, is a museum of photography

[LINK: (MoMu ModeMuseum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ModeMuseum_Antwerpen]  is a fashion museum. Founded on 21 September 2002, the museum collects, conserves, studies and exhibits Belgian fashion.

[LINK: (Red Star Line Museum) -> https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Star_Line_Museum] , is a museum about the history of the  [LINK: (Red Star Line) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Star_Line] , which opened on 28 September 2013.

[LINK: (DIVA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DIVA_Museum_for_Diamonds,_Jewellery_and_Silver]  is a museum about diamonds, jewellery and silver.

[LINK: (Atlantic Wall & Air War Bunker Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Wall_%26_Air_War_Bunker_Museum_Antwerp]  is a military war museum which preserves fortifications of the  [LINK: (Atlantic Wall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Wall] .

[LINK: (Flemish Tram and Bus Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vlaams_Tram-_en_Autobusmuseum]  is a museum of public transport.

The  [LINK: (Maidens' House) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maagdenhuis_Museum]  is an art museum and historical museum located in a 17th-century historic building.

The  [LINK: (Butcher's Hall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vleeshuis]  is a museum focusing on sound, music and dance.

[LINK: (Plantin-Moretus Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plantin-Moretus_Museum]

[LINK: (Royal Museum of Fine Arts (KMSKA)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Antwerp]

[LINK: (Museum of Contemporary Art (M HKA)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_of_Contemporary_Art,_Antwerp]

[LINK: (Museum aan de Stroom) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_aan_de_Stroom]

Antwerp is the home of the  [LINK: (Antwerp Jazz Club (AJC)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_Jazz_Club_(AJC)] , founded in 1938 and located on the square  [LINK: (Grote Markt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grote_Markt_(Antwerp)]  since 1994. [LINK: ([89]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-Den-Bengel-94]  Antwerp also has various concert halls, such as the  [LINK: (Stadsschouwburg) -> https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stadsschouwburg_Antwerpen] , the  [LINK: (Bourlaschouwburg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bourla_Theatre] ,  [LINK: (the Flemish Opera) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vlaamse_Opera] , the Arenbergschouwburg and the  [LINK: (Koningin Elisabethzaal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_Elisabeth_Hall] ; the latter being the home of the  [LINK: (Antwerp Symphony Orchestra) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_Symphony_Orchestra] . Large (pop) performances are often held in the  [LINK: (Sportpaleis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sportpaleis]  or in the  [LINK: (Lotto Arena) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lotto_Arena] . These event halls are located in  [LINK: (Merksem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merksem] . In addition, there are other well-known venues like  [LINK: (De Roma) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Roma]  and  [LINK: (Trix) -> https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trix_(muziekcentrum)] , both of which are located in  [LINK: (Borgerhout) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borgerhout] .

A band originating in Antwerp achieving major international success is  [LINK: (Deus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deus_(band)] .

Some well known festivals around the city are:  [LINK: (Linkerwoofer) -> https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linkerwoofer] , a pop-rock music festival located at the left bank of the  [LINK: (Scheldt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scheldt] . This music festival starts in August and mostly local Belgian musicians play and perform in this event. [LINK: ([90]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-95]  [LINK: ([91]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-96]  [LINK: ([92]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-97]   [LINK: (Jazz Middelheim) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jazz_Middelheim]  is an annual summer  [LINK: (jazz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jazz]  festival in the Middelheim Park.  [LINK: (Tomorrowland) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomorrowland_(festival)]  is probably the most famous festival to arise from Antwerp. Though the festival is effectively located 15 km (10 mi) south of the city in Boom, its founders in the past organised a festival ('Antwerp is burning') within city limits. The office of the company behind Tomorrowland (weareone.world bvba) is located in the heart of the city. The company founders are involved in conceptualising urban planning concepts for specific Antwerp areas and are known to invite their favourite Antwerp food places to set up a pop-up at the festival.  [LINK: (Sfinks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sfinks_Festival]  festival is a global pop festival that takes place annually in  [LINK: (Boechout) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boechout] , a village southeast of Antwerp. The first edition dates from 1976. Other popular festivals are Fire Is Gold, Ampere Open Air and Vaag Outdoor focusing more on hip-hop music, house and techno.

Antwerp is a city that boasts a diverse and vibrant nightlife, with many cafés and nightclubs situated throughout its various neighbourhoods. The old centre and  [LINK: (Grote Markt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grote_Markt_(Antwerp)]  offer a cosy atmosphere surrounded by authentic city buildings and plenty of cafes. The Belgians are well-known for their beer, and visitors can savor a wide range of local brews while enjoying the nightlife.

Other popular areas include het Mechelseplein, home to various cafés as well as the bustling Dageraadplaats in the belle-époque neighbourhood of  [LINK: (Zurenborg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zurenborg] .

The neighbourhood of het  [LINK: (Zuid) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zuid,_Antwerp]  is also a popular nightlife destination, with many bars, restaurants, and cafés. This district is named after its nearby museums, including the  [LINK: (Royal Museum of Fine Arts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Antwerp] , the  [LINK: (Museum of Contemporary Art Antwerp (M HKA)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_of_Contemporary_Art,_Antwerp] , and the  [LINK: (Photo Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fotomuseum_Antwerp] .

Het groen kwartier (the green quarter) is also a hub of cultural and artistic activity. Het Eilandje is a neighbourhood in Antwerp, with many cafés and restaurants situated around the  [LINK: (MAS Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_aan_de_Stroom] .

Antwerp is known for a diverse range of nightclubs that cater to different tastes. Among these clubs are some of the most famous and notorious in Belgium.  [LINK: (Cinderella's Ballroom) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinderella%27s_Ballroom]  was famous in the 1980s.

Antwerp is a rising fashion city, and has produced designers such as the  [LINK: (Antwerp Six) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_Six] . The city has a cult status in the fashion world, due to the  [LINK: (Royal Academy of Fine Arts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Academy_of_Fine_Arts_Antwerp] , one of the most important fashion academies in the world. It has served as the learning centre for many Belgian  [LINK: (fashion designers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fashion_design] . Since the 1980s, several graduates of the Belgian Royal Academy of Fine Arts have become internationally successful fashion designers in Antwerp. The city has had a huge influence on other Belgian fashion designers such as  [LINK: (Raf Simons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raf_Simons] ,  [LINK: (Veronique Branquinho) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veronique_Branquinho] ,  [LINK: (Olivier Theyskens) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olivier_Theyskens]  and  [LINK: (Kris Van Assche) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kris_Van_Assche] . [LINK: ([93]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-98]  In 2019, Arte won the Fashion brand of the Year award at the Belgian Fashion Awards. The Antwerp label was founded by Bertony Da Silva in 2009. [LINK: ([94]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-99]

Antwerp is famous for its local products. In August every year the Bollekesfeest takes place. The Bollekesfeest is a showcase for such local products as Bolleke, an amber beer from the  [LINK: (De Koninck Brewery) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Koninck_Brewery] . The city's historical ale, Seefbier, [LINK: ([95]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-100]  dating back to the 16th century and brewed at the Antwerpse Brouw Compagnie is a testament to the city's long brewing history and one of Belgium's oldest existing beerstyles. The  [LINK: (Mokatine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mokatine&action=edit&redlink=1]  sweets made by  [LINK: (Confiserie Roodthooft) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confiserie_Roodthooft] , Elixir D'Anvers, a locally made liquor, locally roasted  [LINK: (coffee) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coffee]  from Koffie Verheyen, sugar from Candico, Poolster pickled herring and Equinox horse meat, are other examples of local specialities. One of the most known products of the city are its biscuits, the  [LINK: (Antwerpse Handjes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerpse_Handjes] , literally "Antwerp Hands". Usually made from a short pastry with  [LINK: (almonds) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Almond]  or  [LINK: (milk chocolate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milk_chocolate] , they symbolize the Antwerp trademark and folklore. The local products are represented by a non-profit organization, Streekproducten Provincie Antwerpen vzw. [LINK: ([96]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-101]

Antwerp has grown into the culinary capital of Flanders and Belgium. It has no fewer than eleven restaurants with at least one  [LINK: (MICHELIN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michelin_Guide]  star. Zilte by Viki Geunes located in the  [LINK: (MAS museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_aan_de_Stroom]  even received the ultimate award of three stars. [LINK: ([97]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-102]

The city of Antwerp was co-host for the 2021  [LINK: (World Choir Games) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Choir_Games]  together with the city of  [LINK: (Ghent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghent] . [LINK: ([98]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-103]  Organised by the  [LINK: (Interkultur Foundation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Interkultur_Foundation&action=edit&redlink=1] , the World Choir Games is the biggest choral competition and festival in the world.

A number of Christian missions to seafarers are based in Antwerp. These include the  [LINK: (Mission to Seafarers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mission_to_Seafarers] ,  [LINK: (British & International Sailors' Society) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sailors%27_Society] , the  [LINK: (Finnish Seamen's Mission) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finnish_Seamen%27s_Mission] , the Norwegian  [LINK: (Sjømannskirken) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norwegian_Church_Abroad]  and the  [LINK: (Apostleship of the Sea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apostleship_of_the_Sea] . They provide cultural and social activities as well as religious services. The iconic Italiëlei premises have been closed down and all activities have been moved to the Antwerp Harbour Hotel on Noorderlaan.


# SPORT #

Antwerp held the  [LINK: (1920 Summer Olympics) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1920_Summer_Olympics] , which were the first games after the  [LINK: (First World War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_World_War]  and also the only ones to be held in Belgium. [LINK: ([99]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-sports-reference-104]  [LINK: ([100]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-105]  Antwerp hosted the  [LINK: (World Artistic Gymnastics Championships) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Artistic_Gymnastics_Championships]  in  [LINK: (2013) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2013_World_Artistic_Gymnastics_Championships]  and  [LINK: (2023) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_World_Artistic_Gymnastics_Championships]  in the  [LINK: (Sportpaleis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sportpaleis] . Annually Antwerp hosts the  [LINK: (European Open) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Open_(tennis)]  ATP Tour 250 tennis tournament that takes place in  [LINK: (Antwerp's Sport Palace) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sportpaleis] . It was introduced for the  [LINK: (2016 ATP World Tour) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_ATP_World_Tour] . Another annual event is the  [LINK: (Antwerp 10 miles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_10_Miles]  and the  [LINK: (Antwerp marathon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_Marathon] .

For the year 2013, Antwerp was awarded the title of European Capital of Sport.

Antwerp is home of two professional football clubs being,  [LINK: (Royal Antwerp F.C.) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Antwerp_F.C.] , currently playing in the  [LINK: (Belgian First Division) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_First_Division] , founded in 1880 and is known as 'The Great Old' for being the first club registered to the  [LINK: (Royal Belgian Football Association) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Belgian_Football_Association]  in 1895. [LINK: ([101]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-106]  Over the course of the club's history, Royal Antwerp have won five  [LINK: (Belgian league titles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_Pro_League]  as well as four  [LINK: (Belgian Cups) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_Cup] .  [LINK: (Antwerp F.C) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Antwerp_F.C.] . is also a member of the  [LINK: (Club of Pioneers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Club_of_Pioneers] . Another club in the city is  [LINK: (K Beerschot VA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K_Beerschot_VA] , founded in 1899 by former Royal Antwerp players. They play at the  [LINK: (Olympisch Stadion) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olympisch_Stadion_(Antwerp)] , the main venue of the 1920 Olympics. Between these two football teams there has always been a big rivalry. When the two play against each other the stadiums are packed and the passioned fans give a great display of their passion, but this has also led to fights, hooliganism and vandalism.

The  [LINK: (Antwerp Giants) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_Giants]  play in the  [LINK: (BNXT league) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BNXT_League] , the league is the first tier in both the Dutch and Belgian system. Their home ground is the  [LINK: (Lotto Arena) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lotto_Arena] .  Antwerp has won the Belgian championship once, in 2000. The team has also won five Belgian Cups, in 2000, 2007, 2019, 2020 and 2023.

The city's  [LINK: (Groenplaats) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groenplaats]  hosted the official 2022  [LINK: (FIBA 3x3 World Cup) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIBA_3x3_World_Cup] . [LINK: ([102]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp#cite_note-FIBA,_January_2021-107]


# EDUCATION #

Antwerp has a university and several colleges. The  [LINK: (University of Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Antwerp]  (Universiteit Antwerpen) was established in 2003, following the merger of the RUCA, UFSIA and UIA institutes. Their roots go back to 1852. The university has approximately 23,000 registered students, making it the third-largest university in Flanders, as well as 1,800 foreign students. It has 7 faculties, spread over four campus locations in the city centre and in the south of the city. The university is part of  [LINK: (Young Universities for the Future of Europe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Young_Universities_for_the_Future_of_Europe&action=edit&redlink=1]  (YUFE) and  [LINK: (Young European Research Universities Network) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Young_European_Research_Universities_Network]  (YERUN).

The  [LINK: (KU Leuven) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KU_Leuven]  has a presence in Antwerp too.

The city has several colleges, including  [LINK: (Antwerp Management School) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_Management_School]  (AMS), the  [LINK: (Antwerp Maritime Academy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_Maritime_Academy] , the  [LINK: (Karel de Grote University of Applied Sciences and Arts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karel_de_Grote_University_of_Applied_Sciences_and_Arts]  and  [LINK: (AP Hogeschool Antwerpen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artesis_Plantijn_University_College_of_Antwerp] . AP Hogeschool has about 15,000 students and 1,600 staff, and the Karel de Grote Hogeschool has about 13,500 students and 1,300 staff.


# INTERNATIONAL RELATIONS #

The following places are twinned with or are  [LINK: (sister cities) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twin_towns_and_sister_cities]  to Antwerp:

[LINK: (Barcelona) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barcelona] , Spain, 1997

[LINK: (Cape Town) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Town] , South Africa, 1996

[LINK: (Fez) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fez,_Morocco] , Morocco, 2000

[LINK: (Haifa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haifa] , Israel, 1995

[LINK: (Ludwigshafen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ludwigshafen] , Germany, 1998

[LINK: (Marseille) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marseille] , France, 1958

[LINK: (Mulhouse) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mulhouse] , France, 1954

[LINK: (Rostock) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rostock] , Germany, 1963

[LINK: (Rotterdam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rotterdam] , Netherlands, 1940

[LINK: (Shanghai) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shanghai] , China, 1984

[LINK: (Port of Nagoya) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_of_Nagoya] , Japan, 1988

Within the context of development cooperation, Antwerp is also linked to

[LINK: (Paramaribo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paramaribo] , Suriname

[LINK: (Durban) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durban] , South Africa


